course: biology
lesson: 221
title: Pigeons: head bobbing, homing and city cliffs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The grey pigeons on every town square are the grandchildren of wild rock doves, birds that nest on sea cliffs, and to a pigeon a tall building is simply a cliff with windows.
explain: Watch one walk across a station floor: its head seems to jerk back and forth, but really the head stays perfectly still for a moment while the body catches up, so the bird can see the world without blur.
explain: People have kept pigeons for thousands of years, for food, for racing and for carrying messages, because a homing pigeon taken far away will fly straight back to its own loft.
explain: Pigeons are smarter than they look: they can tell people's faces apart and remember who chased them and who fed them.
explain: So the next time someone calls them rats with wings, remember they are old partners of people, living in the cliffs we built.
fact: When a pigeon walks, its head holds still for a moment at each step and then darts forward, which keeps its view steady; a pigeon on a treadmill does not bob, because the world around it is not moving.
fact: Homing pigeons can find their way back to their loft from hundreds of kilometres away; in the First World War a pigeon called Cher Ami carried a message that helped save nearly two hundred soldiers.
fact: City pigeons come from wild rock doves, which still nest on sea cliffs in places like Scotland and Ireland; buildings and ledges are their cliffs.
fact: A pigeon drinks by sucking water up as if through a straw, without tipping its head back like most birds, so it can drink fast and keep watch.
fact: Baby pigeons are rarely seen because the chicks stay hidden in the nest until they are almost grown; both parents feed them a special crop milk.
fact: City pigeons often get string or hair tangled round their toes, which can cut into the foot; wildlife rescuers carefully remove it.
fact: Pigeons can tell people apart by their faces, and in experiments they even learned to tell paintings by different artists apart.
fact: Calling pigeons rats with wings is unfair: they have lived alongside people for thousands of years, and people bred them on purpose.
fact: A male pigeon puffs out his neck, coos and turns in circles to show off to a female; his neck feathers shine green and purple in the light.
fact: Feeding huge crowds of pigeons with bread makes too many pigeons, and birds packed in crowds spread mess and illness more easily.
fact: Pigeons often clap their wings together loudly as they take off, and males also clap in display flights.
exercise: What really happens to a pigeon's head when it walks? | It holds still for a moment, then darts forward | It shakes because the bird is nervous of people | It bobs to push the bird along like an oar in water | It wobbles because its neck is weak
exercise: Why does a pigeon on a treadmill stop bobbing its head? | The world around it is not moving past | The treadmill makes it far too tired to bob | It cannot walk on a moving belt | It is too scared to move its neck at all
exercise: What can a homing pigeon do? | Fly back to its own loft from far away | Find any house you write on a letter | Fly round the whole world without stopping | Follow its owner to a new town
exercise: Which wild bird do city pigeons come from? | The rock dove of sea cliffs | The wood pigeon of deep forests | The seagull of sandy beaches | The parrot of rainforests
exercise: Why do pigeons live so happily on buildings and ledges? | Buildings are like the cliffs their wild ancestors used | They are afraid of walking about on the ground | Trees in towns are too small for them | They like the warmth that comes out of windows, and nothing else
exercise: How does a pigeon drink? | It sucks it up like a straw | It scoops water and tips its head back | It licks water with its tongue | It soaks water up through its feet
exercise: Why do we hardly ever see baby pigeons? | The chicks stay hidden in the nest until almost grown | Pigeons hatch out of the egg already adult-sized | Pigeon chicks live underground | Pigeon chicks are all kept in lofts by people until they are grown up
exercise: What do pigeon parents feed their young chicks? | Crop milk made by both parents | Cow's milk they find in bins | Only whole seeds from the start | Insects carried in their feet
exercise: A pigeon limps with string wound round its toes. What is the best help? | Tell a wildlife rescue, who can remove it carefully | Pull hard on the string yourself, as fast as you can | Leave it, because string falls off by itself in time | Chase it so it shakes the string free
exercise: Can pigeons tell people apart? | Yes, they can recognise faces | No, all people look the same to them | Only if people wear bright colours | Only their owners, by their smell
exercise: Is it fair to call pigeons rats with wings? | No, they have lived with people for thousands of years | Yes, pigeons really are close relatives of rats and mice | Yes, pigeons are the dirtiest of all birds | No, because pigeons cannot carry anything at all
exercise: A pigeon puffs out his neck, coos and turns in circles. What is he doing? | Showing off to a female | Trying to scare away a cat | Getting ready to go to sleep | Choking on a crumb of bread
exercise: Why is throwing heaps of bread to pigeons a bad idea? | It makes too many pigeons that spread mess and illness | Bread makes pigeons fly far too fast | Pigeons cannot swallow bread at all | Bread turns pigeons' grey feathers white, so they cannot hide from hawks
exercise: What is the loud clapping sound when a pigeon takes off? | Its wings hitting together | Its beak snapping shut hard | Its feet stamping on the ledge | Its heart beating very fast
exercise: What colours shine on a male pigeon's neck in the light? | Green and purple | Red and bright yellow | Bright blue and orange | Pure white and black
test: «pigeon in the station keeps nodding its head like it's listening to music lol» What is really going on? | Its head holds still at each step so its view stays steady | It really is nodding along to the music in the station | It is trying to shake off fleas | It is bobbing to keep its balance while it stands on one leg
test: Count them: 6 pigeons stand on a ledge. How many legs and how many wings? | 12 legs and 12 wings | 6 legs and 12 wings | 12 legs and 6 wings | 24 legs and 12 wings
test: «my mate reckons pigeons are just flying rats, they're gross» What is the best answer? | Unfair: they came from cliff doves and have lived with people for thousands of years | Right, pigeons are a kind of rat | Right, pigeons carry every illness there is and are the dirtiest animals in any town | Wrong, pigeons are the same as parrots
test: What would happen if you took a homing pigeon 300 kilometres away and let it go? | It would most likely fly back to its own loft | It would stay where it was let go | It would fly to the nearest big city | It would follow the first car it saw
test: In a cartoon a pigeon tips its head right back to gulp water like a hen. What is true? | Real pigeons suck water up without lifting their heads | Real pigeons drink exactly like hens do, tipping their heads back | Pigeons never drink, they get all their water from bread | Pigeons drink only rainwater, catching drops from the air
test: «never seen a baby pigeon in my life, do they even exist» What is right? | Yes, but the chicks hide in the nest until they are almost grown | No, pigeons are made in factories | No, pigeons lay eggs that hatch straight into grown-up birds | Yes, but they live only in the countryside, far away from towns
test: A girl feeds the same pigeons every day; a boy chases them. Next week, what might the pigeons do? | Come to the girl and fly off from the boy | Treat both children the same way | Follow the boy because he is fast | Forget both of them by the next day
test: «loads of crumbs for all the pigeons, the more the better yeah?» What is best? | No, too much food makes too many pigeons and spreads illness | Yes, pigeons need bread every day to stay healthy | Yes, bread makes pigeons cleaner | No, pigeons hate bread and never eat it, they only eat seeds
test: Count them: a pair of pigeons has 2 chicks in the nest. How many beaks and how many feet altogether? | 4 beaks and 8 feet | 2 beaks and 8 feet | 4 beaks and 4 feet | 4 beaks and 16 feet
test: What would a pigeon most likely use as a nest site if a town had no buildings? | A ledge on a cliff, like its wild ancestors | A hole dug in the ground | The top of a tall grass stem | A floating nest on a lake
test: «heard a pigeon saved soldiers in a war, that's made up surely» What is right? | It is true; Cher Ami carried a message that helped save nearly two hundred soldiers | It is a made-up film story | It is half true; pigeons fought in wars, but only with tiny swords tied on | It is made up, because only dogs ever carried messages in wars
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the head of the pigeon appears to move rhythmically during locomotion. | The head is held stationary during each step, thereby stabilising the visual field | The head is propelled forward to assist the motion of the body | The movement results from a deficiency in the musculature of the neck, common to all pigeons | The movement constitutes a response to audible rhythm
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ancestry of the urban pigeon? | It is descended from the rock dove, a species nesting upon sea cliffs | It is descended from the wood pigeon of deciduous forests | It is descended from gulls domesticated in harbours | It is a species which originated within cities and has no wild ancestor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the drinking behaviour of the pigeon. | Water is drawn upward by suction without elevation of the head | Water is scooped and swallowed by elevation of the head | Water is absorbed exclusively through the skin | Water is obtained solely from food and never drunk
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the young of the pigeon are seldom observed by the public. | The young remain concealed within the nest until nearly fully grown | The young are hatched in a fully adult condition | The young are reared exclusively beneath the ground | The young are removed at hatching by human keepers and reared in lofts
